WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.451 --> 00:00:06.180
APARNA: All right,
can we get started?

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.080
Great.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.310
Good afternoon, and
welcome to IO 313.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.490
There are a lot of amazing
product announcements

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.540
this morning.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.420
And hopefully you noticed that
committed use discounts can

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.520
be used with Container Engine.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.940
And now you can use
Spanner and PostgreSQL also

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.850
in your applications
with Container Engine.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.860
There were not a lot of
Container Engine related

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.411
announcements, and that's
what this talk is for.

00:00:31.411 --> 00:00:32.910
We're going to be
talking about some

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.618
of the latest features
that are coming up

00:00:34.618 --> 00:00:38.740
in our next release, which
is due in early April.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.720
So some of this material is
roadmap and subject to change

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.210
but we're excited to share
with you a preview of some

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:50.550
of the coolest features
that are upcoming

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.190
So one of the highlights
of working in technology,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.840
is when you're
building a product that

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.110
is used by other
products that you love.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.290
And that's been the case
for me with Philips Hue.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.050
Kelsey and I, of course, work on
Kubernetes and Container Engine

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.750
here at Google, but we
are delighted to be joined

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.470
by George Yianni, who is
an inventor of Philips Hue

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.020
lighting, this is IoT
connected lighting.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.320
I've been working with George
for the last two years,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.690
and in close
collaboration with Google,

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.050
but even before that I had
bought Philips Hue lights,

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.270
and I have them installed
in both my living room,

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.390
as well as my kids' bedroom.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.150
And they love the colorful
lights at night, and especially

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.430
on holidays like Halloween.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.820
And now, knowing that
Philips Hue light is powered

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.150
by Google Container Engine, a
product that I helped build,

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.380
makes mommy very cool.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.500
So it's not just
Philips though, you

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.670
might be surprised by how
often you are using Kubernetes.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:53.130
I'd like to do a quick
show of hands here.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:57.830
How many of you have recently
played a hit mobile game,

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.440
or shopped at one of the
world's largest retailers,

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.189
or followed breaking news,
or attended a concert?

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:05.480
And then I put the last one
in here for the workaholics

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.010
amongst us, how many of you
have filed expenses recently?

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.117
Anyone who hasn't
done any of these?

00:02:13.117 --> 00:02:15.450
I think those people have
been busy making contributions

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.180
to the Kubernetes
open source codebase,

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.670
and they haven't
done any of these.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.840
But as you can guess,
all of these services

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.760
are running on Kubernetes today.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.490
And that's just us that's
just a small sampling.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.580
So you might ask,
why is this the case?

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.300
I think there are three reasons.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.640
Number one is speed.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.790
And here, I'm talking about
developer productivity.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.190
These are quotes from
some of our users.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:45.810
They've moved from commit
to production in minutes,

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.450
and they do that 20
to 50 times a day.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.460
That's number one.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.320
Number two is scalability.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.810
And here, it's really
efficient scale out.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:58.410
So Pokemon Go the fastest
app to $1 billion,

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:02.040
and Kubernetes was behind
the scaling of that app.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.810
Also Black Friday
demand, Home Depot

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:07.770
talked about it in yesterday's
keynote, also powered

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.120
by Kubernetes.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.480
And what tends to happen is that
then becomes the new standard.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.640
When a technology
becomes this commonplace,

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.920
everyone expects
all applications

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.520
to be able to meet this
type of scalability,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.930
and this type of
development speed.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.300
And last but not
least, it's open.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.560
So you can use
Kubernetes anywhere,

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.440
whether it's on Google's
cloud, or it's on your premise.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.310
And Walmart is a very
nice example of that.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.480
Walmart are using Kubernetes in
their logistics warehouses, 200

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.960
of these warehouses.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:42.780
These are small, IT closets,
almost, with VMware on them

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.844
and they're running
Kubernetes on that.

00:03:44.844 --> 00:03:46.260
And of course,
there are many that

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.080
are using it on Google
Cloud, as well as on AWS.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:52.110
So that flexibility is something
that hopefully many of you

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.700
value.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.560
But there's nothing
better than hearing

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:01.290
about an actual deployment
from an actual user.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.140
What's it like to use
Kubernetes on Google's cloud?

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.700
Is it easy, is it
secure, is it reliable?

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.640
So for that, I'd like
to, without further ado,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.400
welcome George Yianni
to come up on the stage

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.160
and talk about Philips Hue
and how they're using GKE.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:23.610
GEORGE: Thank you
very much, Aparna.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.610
Now, I work for
Philips Lighting.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:32.510
And we're actually the largest
lighting company in the world.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.360
And I think it's almost certain
that every single person

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.360
inside this room, has
actually experienced

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.350
some of our products.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:45.960
Whether it's in our homes,
under the street lights, which

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.420
lights our roads,
all admiring one

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:51.850
of the thousands of monuments,
like San Francisco's Bay

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:55.410
Bridge, which we actually
do the lighting for.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:01.170
Now Philips Lighting is
really driving for a change

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.000
in the lighting industry.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.230
And that change is really
to tap into the value

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.650
that lighting being
ubiquitously all around us,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.010
can potentially bring.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:17.570
There is nowhere where
we work, travel, or enjoy

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:20.420
ourselves which is an underneath
multiple artificial light

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:21.540
points.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.350
And we think that, by making
those light points more

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:29.420
intelligent, we can make the
world more personalizable,

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:33.410
easier to manage, and
easier to monitor.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.890
Now our answer for
this transformation,

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:39.800
in the consumer space, is
my product, Phillip Hue,

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.320
which I been working on for
the last five or six years.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:49.160
It is one of the most
popular smart home products.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.480
And we really believe that by
making lighting in our homes

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:55.130
more connected, we
can really transform

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:58.470
what it does in our lives.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:02.220
I believe that lighting can
be an alternative to having

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.030
a gadara home, by making it
seem like you're at home,

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.920
even when you're on vacation.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.490
I believe that lighting can be
an alternative to your morning

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:14.460
cup of coffee.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.350
By tapping into
the way our bodies

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:20.050
react to warm and cold light.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:23.050
I believe that lighting is
an alternative to painting

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:26.140
your walls, by letting
you change the look

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:30.160
and feel of your rooms
multiple times a day,

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.980
to suit different activities.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:36.130
I even think that,
in the future,

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:40.930
lighting will be an alternative
to surround sound speakers,

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:44.170
by having your lights
synchronized content on a TV

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:47.800
screen and bringing the
explosions, or the forest,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.830
into your entire room in
synchronization with what's

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:52.480
going on the screen.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.080
And we can do all of this
by making your lives more

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:59.780
comfortable and convenient.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:02.510
There is untold potential
for smart lighting

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.430
to change how we live our lives.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:11.580
Now, why do we need a cloud
solution to do all of this?

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.940
And actually, right from the
beginning of Philips Hue,

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.530
we designed a cloud
backend for our solution.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:21.660
And we actually designed
it not as the primary way,

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.300
we focused as much as possible
to what actually happen locally

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.080
inside the home to keep
latency as low as possible,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.330
but we always said we
need a cloud backend.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:32.640
And we need it so that
we can let people control

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.690
and monitor their homes
when they're away.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:40.740
We need it so that we can keep
all of our products up to date

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.770
the latest features
in security updates.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:47.901
We need it so that we can
learn from our consumers

00:07:47.901 --> 00:07:50.400
how people are actually using
this product so we can make it

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.930
better over time.

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.860
And we knew we needed it
to be able to integrate

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.490
with other cloud services
and platforms that

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.650
are out there to
realize new use cases.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.941
Like, for example, with
if this then that, where

00:08:04.941 --> 00:08:07.440
we can have your lights flash
when your favorite sports team

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:07.940
scores.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:15.690
Now, we also have
some particular needs

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:20.850
that drove our choice in
design for Cloud solution.

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.160
One of the most
important things to us

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:29.210
is the incremental cost
per new home we serve.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:31.630
And this is because
all the cloud services

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:35.049
to Philips Hue, we bundle
is a free service when

00:08:35.049 --> 00:08:36.520
you buy your first new product.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:44.190
That means the lifetime
cost to offer these services

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:47.280
towards that customer
have to be bundled

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:48.990
in the bill of materials
of that product.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.230
So in order to keep our products
as affordable as possible--

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:58.440
we are obsessed with
making that cost

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.930
to offer this free service
as cheap as possible.

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:04.360
And it's really this incremental
cost for every new user.

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.530
Another thing which really
drove us, especially

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:11.890
at the beginning, was--

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:14.990
Philips Hue, we launched
it back in 2012,

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:18.400
was really a
new-to-world product.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.040
And frankly, a lot
of people thought

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.790
we were a bit crazy
making a $60 lightbulb you

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:26.190
could connect to the internet.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:29.180
Now, we believed that this
would become a huge thing,

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.489
but we didn't know
how fast it would.

00:09:31.489 --> 00:09:33.030
We didn't know if
this is going to be

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.100
a very slow ramp
up, or something

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.780
that took off immediately.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:41.860
And we really risked, in
building this cloud backend,

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:46.210
that we had a solution, which
either had the potential

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:50.170
to kill this small idea
early on by lumbering it

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:55.810
with expensive infrastructure,
and operations costs,

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:58.150
or, on the other side, that
couldn't actually keep up

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:01.479
if the market did explode.

00:10:01.479 --> 00:10:03.770
That's one of the major
reasons we came to Google Cloud

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:06.394
when we were ready and built our
first infrastructure on Google

00:10:06.394 --> 00:10:09.750
App Engine, because it let us
pay for what we needed, and let

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:12.990
us actually scale
when, luckily for us,

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.980
the market did explode
and continues to explode.

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:20.160
And we've actually had a very
surprisingly pleasant journey

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:23.790
in scaling our solution
multiple factors every year.

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:31.150
Another thing, we're
not a cloud company,

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.144
we're a lighting company.

00:10:33.144 --> 00:10:34.560
What we do, and
what drives us, is

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.200
to offer the best possible
lighting use cases

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.280
and experiences to consumers.

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:42.500
And that's what I won't
spend my engineering money.

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:45.500
So I don't want to have to spend
all of my engineering money

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:49.420
on keeping infrastructure
up and running.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:50.980
So I really wanted
a solution which

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.862
let me focus on actually
building our use cases out,

00:10:54.862 --> 00:10:57.070
and not having to worry
about maintaining and running

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:58.722
infrastructure.

00:10:58.722 --> 00:11:00.680
And finally, and this
one is actually a bit new

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:03.890
and wasn't something we had
in mind at the beginning,

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.440
is latency is super important.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:08.870
When we first
designed all of this,

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.550
the idea was, OK,
you're controlling

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:14.970
your lights and outside of
home to update your schedules,

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:16.940
or check that you haven't
left the lights on.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.790
It doesn't matter if it takes
a few seconds to do that.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:22.160
But what's happening
more and more,

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:25.520
is other smart home products
are entering your home which

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:28.400
are cloud centric.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.880
All of their logic intelligence,
APIs, sitting in the cloud.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.890
And so we integrate
them also in the cloud.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:37.590
So an integration, like what
we have with the Google Voice

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:40.720
Assistant, that actually
goes up to the Google

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:44.420
Cloud for the voice processing,
and then pokes our cloud APIs.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:47.450
And this is something you're
doing inside your home.

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:51.200
And if your lights take more
than the second turn on,

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.150
that feels really slow.

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:56.480
So we have a new, renewed
push to actually get our cloud

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:59.300
latencies down as much as
possible, because it's actually

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:02.480
being used for real time
use cases inside the home.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:10.360
Now, we have a very broad and
rich partnership with Google.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:12.950
And it's one of the great
things about actually working

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:14.120
with Google.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:18.260
We use a broad
variety of products.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:23.490
We use a whole suite of products
from that cloud platform

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.680
together with Apigee,
to actually build up

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.630
cloud backend.

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.450
We build our own
Android app, and have

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:34.050
hundreds of other apps
built with our lighting APIs

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:36.780
running on Android
and Android Wear.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:41.030
We were one of the very early
works with Nesse partners,

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:45.410
letting us offer use
cases to, for example,

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:49.900
reacts when a Nest Protect
Smoke alarm, detects smoke,

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:52.360
by setting your lights
to a dim red color, which

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:57.350
actually helps you see better
through white smoke conditions.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:02.510
And we were also launch partners
for Google's new offerings

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:06.290
in the smartphone space, with
Google Home, Google Assistant,

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:10.490
and Google Wi-Fi, where we
also have great integrations.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.650
So there's a lot of
touch points with Google.

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:14.600
And we really work
across the company

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:17.630
to achieve our mission of
letting people use lighting

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.990
in a richer way.

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:23.330
So what does this
actually look like?

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:27.430
So this is a snapshot, a
very cloud centric snapshot,

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:30.730
of how the new
architecture looks.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:35.920
And we'll go through what
happens with a simple command.

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:39.145
Let's say you've taken
out your Android phone,

00:13:39.145 --> 00:13:41.020
and you've asked Google
Assistant to turn off

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:42.790
your lights.

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:46.309
Something that happens
maybe not millions of times

00:13:46.309 --> 00:13:47.600
a day, but lots of times a day.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:53.290
What that will do, is that
will initiate a client

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:58.180
request using an API, to turn
off all lights inside the home.

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:02.520
That goes initially
towards Apigee,

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:06.270
which is our entry point towards
the cloud for clients, which

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:10.410
will check that that application
has the right permissions

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:15.350
to use APIs it's requesting,
and is given its quotas.

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:20.100
That then gets passed on
to a set of Kubernetes pods

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:24.290
that you call client Eastwood
These are the pods which handle

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:27.140
all of our clients requests,
they process that request,

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:30.590
and then they forward it on
towards the appropriate pod,

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:33.710
which is handling the connection
towards that particular home

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:35.360
that's being addressed.

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:38.550
And we do that forwarding
via Redis instance

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.380
that's running on
Compute Engine.

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:44.740
Now we actually have
two different ways

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:46.570
of interacting with
Hue homes, and that's

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:49.900
because we're in the
process of transitioning

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:53.290
towards a new, lower
latency solution.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.450
So the legacy
mechanism, which will

00:14:55.450 --> 00:15:00.190
stay relevant for a good
time yet, is GoldenGate.

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:01.900
And this you can
think of effectively

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:06.550
as a message queue, where all
messages for bridges to process

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:09.520
are stored, and it can be
pulled by bridges, when they're

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:13.989
triggered to do so, actually
fetch and processes messages.

00:15:13.989 --> 00:15:15.780
The new solution that
we've come up with we

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:18.960
call Freddie Mercury.

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:23.520
And this actually maintains
permanent open WebSocket

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:27.420
connections towards every
home, concurrently, 24/7,

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:29.000
in the world.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:31.430
And by doing this,
we are actually

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:36.440
able to push messages directly
into the homes with extremely

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:38.480
faster latency,
so we can actually

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:42.440
get much closer to real time
performance inside the home.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:45.740
And we're rolling out
supports for this capability

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:50.250
towards bridges around
the world as we speak.

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:53.790
Now this Freddie Mercury
pod, actually, we

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:56.730
needed to develop
a new, custom auto

00:15:56.730 --> 00:16:00.240
scaler to be able to get that
to work and scale reliably,

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:04.710
because we actually run
into limits of number

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:06.240
of open connections
on these pods

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.210
before we had any
memory or CPU limits.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:11.770
So we have a custom
Autoscaler that is doing that.

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:14.760
Now, with either of
those pods, a message

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:16.979
will eventually arrive
into our bridge, which

00:16:16.979 --> 00:16:19.020
is a central device we
have inside the home, that

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:21.630
manages the lighting
network inside the home.

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:23.310
That bridge will
process it, and then

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:27.060
initiate a Zogby
lightlink radio broadcast

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:30.030
inside the home, which we
picked up by all of the lights,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:32.460
and then they will turn off.

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:35.480
And if we've done our
job right, and we're

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:37.940
using all new fancy
service, all of that

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:41.110
should happen in
less than a second.

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:49.160
So this is what it takes in
2017 to turn off a light bulb.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:51.860
Next to that we have a
bunch of other services.

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:55.070
We have front-end, where
users can create and manage

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:57.980
accounts, monitor their homes,
which is actually built largely

00:16:57.980 --> 00:16:59.599
on App Engine.

00:16:59.599 --> 00:17:00.890
As well as a few of the things.

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:03.560
We do a lot of our
logging and analytics

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:06.327
and Datadog, which gives
us a really great overview

00:17:06.327 --> 00:17:07.910
snapshot of everything
that's going on

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:09.770
within our infrastructure.

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:14.599
So this is more or less how
the Hue cloud is looking today.

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:19.490
So how is actually been
using Google Container

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:21.560
Engine in production?

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.900
I think the first
thing is, we've really

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:26.490
enjoyed using the technology.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:32.730
It's really given us the
right balance between freedom

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:35.310
to actually optimize
and design our systems

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:40.220
for our particular needs that
we didn't have with App Engine.

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:44.270
But it's also not giving us
too much additional overhead

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:47.390
and burden in terms of
actually running the system,

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:50.450
and he's really given
us the confidence

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:54.530
to go all in with
small, micro-services

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:58.310
that we can run at scale,
allowing us to actually develop

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:00.610
and launch much more quickly
because we have a much

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:01.860
better separation of concerns.

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:06.816
So, all in all,
that's good stuff.

00:18:06.816 --> 00:18:08.190
We're focusing on
features, we're

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:10.590
developing faster with
scaling, without having

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:12.780
to worry about infrastructure.

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:15.780
It hasn't been
without pain points.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.780
It was quite some
work to actually make

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:22.290
the transition from App
Engine towards Kubernetes,

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:25.290
and some of the challenges
that we've had particularly

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:28.320
around the logging
infrastructure,

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:32.730
around GKE, where we had
quite some issues in terms

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:36.420
of getting reliable
logging out of the system

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.642
when pods were being
used intensively,

00:18:39.642 --> 00:18:40.600
and we were developing.

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:42.641
So putting a lot of logging
in to understand what

00:18:42.641 --> 00:18:44.160
was going on.

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:46.074
These things are now
largely resolved,

00:18:46.074 --> 00:18:47.490
but it did make
some of the issues

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:51.710
that we faced early on a bit
more difficult to diagnose.

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:55.100
Another thing, something
that we're focusing on more,

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:56.670
is lower latency.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:59.120
So we're looking at
how we can actually

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:00.590
have our infrastructure
replicated

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:03.560
in more places in the world,
just to get latencies down

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:06.380
so we don't have to cross
the Atlantic so often.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:09.470
And that's something that we've
been reluctant to do up to now,

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:12.680
because it will increase the
amount of operational overhead

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:15.500
that we have, and my
developers say, no,

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:18.360
when I tell him about that.

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:20.320
But all in all, it's
been a great experience.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:24.840
And we continue to enjoy Aparna
and the rest of the Kubernetes

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:26.030
team.

00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:27.196
Do you want to come back up?

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:36.600
APARNA: Thank you,
George, that was great.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:45.450
So, we have some features, in
our upcoming release in fact.

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:48.630
Philips is looking to
go to multi-regions

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:51.330
with their deployment, to lower
latencies in other regions

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.160
as well, and we
have some features

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:57.140
coming in our latest release
that should help with that.

00:19:57.140 --> 00:19:58.880
And while specific
features are important

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:01.280
to specific customers,
I think one thing

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:05.060
that is important to all
customers is our velocity.

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:08.930
This chart is showing we
have had a consistent release

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:11.390
of Container Engine
and Kubernetes

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:13.760
every quarter over
the last year,

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:16.620
and we're going
to continue that.

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:18.310
So, the good news
is that if there

00:20:18.310 --> 00:20:21.440
is a feature that
didn't get in this week,

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:25.380
it'll likely be
there in 12 weeks.

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.780
And you can contribute
to this project,

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:31.150
including contributing
to the code,

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:34.470
but also contributing use
cases, and what you'd like

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:37.110
to see in the features roadmap.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:42.180
So, each release is not just
an effort of Google engineers,

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:45.150
but also our community
of users, and the broader

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:48.160
community of companies
that work on Kubernetes.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:51.690
And overall, more than
442 years of effort

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:55.470
have gone into Kubernetes
and Container Engine.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.670
So now, let's talk about
our upcoming release,

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:01.140
and what are the features that
we'd like to preview today.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:05.450
As a reminder, this is roadmap,
so this is subject to change.

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:08.860
First and foremost, is scale.

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:11.560
Scale tends to be
important to a lot of you.

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:14.880
And we will be announcing,
Kubernetes will be announcing,

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:18.420
support for 5,000 node clusters
in the upcoming release

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:20.320
at the end of this month.

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:23.520
That means that a single
Kubernetes control

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:29.880
plane will be able to
control 5000 nodes, 5000 vms,

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:34.210
in the case of Container Engine,
and up to 30 pods per node.

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:37.800
So that gets you more than
100,000 pods per cluster.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:39.460
That's a fairly large cluster.

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:43.480
You can put a lot of
services into that.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:46.450
And along with that, we will
have sophisticated scheduling,

00:21:46.450 --> 00:21:48.010
some of which I'll
talk about, and

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:50.170
sophisticated multi-tenancy
features, which

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.540
are on the roadmap as well.

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:56.640
Here's kind of the
history of scale.

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:59.940
You know, it was particularly
important to Pokemon Go

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:04.090
that we were working on
this when they came along.

00:22:04.090 --> 00:22:07.670
And as you can see, Kubernetes
and Container Engine

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:10.530
started at a humble
100 pods per node,

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:14.620
and over the last two years
has now scaled up to 5,000.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:17.510
Every time, the metric that
we've used to measure scale

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:21.380
is the same, which is that
99th percentile API latency is

00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:24.890
less than one second, and the
99th percentile pod startup

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:27.480
time is less than five seconds.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:29.360
And this has been tested
in our environment,

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:31.580
and verified for
5,000 node clusters.

00:22:31.580 --> 00:22:33.770
And we look forward to
supporting production

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:36.980
applications on this.

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:39.290
So, you know, much
more practical,

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:42.020
scale is a good thing,
but you're not necessarily

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:45.080
going to manually scale
to a large number.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:47.240
A lot of customers
use auto scaling,

00:22:47.240 --> 00:22:49.670
and George mentioned that
they use auto scaling.

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:51.950
There are two types of
auto scaling in Kubernetes

00:22:51.950 --> 00:22:55.880
and Container Engine, one is
horizontal pod auto scaling.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:57.380
This has been
around for a while.

00:22:57.380 --> 00:22:59.250
It's very easy to set up.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:03.080
You can configure it for a
replica set or a deployment,

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:06.050
and you basically use
this command line.

00:23:06.050 --> 00:23:08.960
You set a minimum and
a maximum, in terms

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:10.490
of the nodes that you want.

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:15.110
And currently, the target
that the system optimizes for

00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:17.169
is CPU utilization.

00:23:17.169 --> 00:23:18.710
This is not always
the thing that you

00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:20.730
want to use that you
want to optimize for.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.880
So one of the roadmap
items we're working on

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:25.579
is built in support
for custom metrics.

00:23:25.579 --> 00:23:27.620
This is something, though,
that you can do today,

00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:29.480
and as George
mentioned, they are

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:33.350
using a number of WebSocket
requests, or queue length,

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:36.230
as a custom metric to
feed into the auto scaler.

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:37.730
So that's something
that you can do,

00:23:37.730 --> 00:23:41.150
it's not a feature yet, a
fully supported feature yet,

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:42.529
but many people do use it.

00:23:42.529 --> 00:23:45.070
And that's one of the beautiful
things about Container Engine

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:48.940
and Kubernetes is the ways
that you can customize it.

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:51.250
The second feature
on auto scaling

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:53.020
is cluster auto scaling.

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:56.230
So, you have a cluster, you
can scale the number of pods

00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:59.380
but what if a pod cannot be
scheduled because the cluster

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:01.030
is too full.

00:24:01.030 --> 00:24:03.310
That's where a cluster
auto scaling comes in.

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:09.490
And we increase the size
of the pool by adding vms,

00:24:09.490 --> 00:24:10.570
when necessary.

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:13.660
And when there's not enough
utilization on the vms,

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:15.130
so we decrease the skies.

00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:17.230
So it's both scale
up, and scale down.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:20.800
And again, that can be set
up with a simple command

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:24.640
specifying the minimum and
maximum nodes that you want.

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:27.640
I'm curious, how many of you are
using any of these auto scaling

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:30.639
features today?

00:24:30.639 --> 00:24:31.180
That's great.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.880
Yeah, I hear very positive
things about this.

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:35.530
Cluster auto-scaling
is beta, and we're

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:38.710
adding a lot more features to
support bigger clusters, so

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:40.510
expect that to go
GA in the next year.

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:45.770
The final point around
scaling is when you

00:24:45.770 --> 00:24:48.220
want to go to multiple regions.

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:51.980
Currently, Kubernetes clusters
can span multiple zones,

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:53.600
and we'll talk
about how to set up

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:56.990
a multi-zone cluster
in the next portion,

00:24:56.990 --> 00:25:00.260
but if you want to have a
presence in another region,

00:25:00.260 --> 00:25:03.790
say in Europe and
in North America,

00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:06.470
and you want to have a local
cluster another region, then

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:09.410
you go ahead and you set up
a cluster in that region.

00:25:09.410 --> 00:25:12.470
With cluster federation, you
can manage those two clusters,

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:14.660
whether they are in
different regions or actually

00:25:14.660 --> 00:25:18.680
in different clouds,
from a single API.

00:25:18.680 --> 00:25:22.012
And that's done by setting
up a Federated control plane.

00:25:22.012 --> 00:25:24.470
And these are some of the things
that the Federated control

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:25.580
plane offers.

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:29.600
It offers an API that's very
similar to the cluster API,

00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:32.390
in fact it has all
of the same objects.

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:35.660
And in the upcoming release,
we will be announcing support

00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:38.240
for deployments in
the federation API

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:41.570
as well as for DaemonSets.

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:45.020
So that means that
from one API endpoint,

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:47.630
you can deploy across
clusters in multiple regions,

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:50.290
or potentially multiple clouds.

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:53.977
And we continue to add
to that federation API.

00:25:53.977 --> 00:25:55.810
The other things that
the control plane does

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:59.230
is check the health of the
cluster's, and also offers GEO

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:01.110
load balancing in GCP only.

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:09.140
So while scaling, and
auto-scaling, and federation

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:11.000
have been a big
part of what you've

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:13.670
come to expect from
Kubernetes, another thing that

00:26:13.670 --> 00:26:16.070
has been top of mind
for many of our users,

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:18.297
is Stateful applications.

00:26:18.297 --> 00:26:20.380
How can I run Stateful
applications in containers?

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:23.000
Should I run Stateful
applications in containers?

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:25.060
That's a common question.

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:29.590
Dynamic storage provisioning is
going GA in the next release.

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:31.240
And we're going
to talk about how

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:34.090
to use Stateful applications
along with this.

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:37.810
A common misconception is
that containers are ephemeral,

00:26:37.810 --> 00:26:42.370
and therefore, they're are only
good for stateless web serving

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:44.050
applications.

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:48.010
Kubernetes actually previsions
the storage automatically

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:50.770
for you, and with dynamic
storage provisioning,

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:53.080
it previsions the
storage dynamically when

00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:57.580
a pod comes up, and it maintains
and persists that storage

00:26:57.580 --> 00:27:02.140
until the application
needs that storage.

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:08.160
Let me talk a little bit more
about that provision storage.

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:11.100
One method is to specify,
in your pod spec,

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:13.080
that there's a
volume, and you'd like

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:15.360
to have that volume mounted.

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:20.190
And in this case, we say
by name what the volume is.

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:23.160
It's a GCE persistent disk,
its name is panda-disk,

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:25.320
and that's the type
of file system it has.

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:28.950
This is an OK methodology,
but it's static.

00:27:28.950 --> 00:27:31.800
First of all, it assumes
that the storage, the volume,

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:32.596
already exists.

00:27:32.596 --> 00:27:35.220
So someone has to pre-provision
that even though the pod hasn't

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:39.170
been provisioned yet,
that's a potential waste.

00:27:39.170 --> 00:27:41.420
Secondly it's not portable.

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:44.480
So if you were to take those
pods back somewhere else,

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:47.810
and that place didn't have
as you see a persistent disk,

00:27:47.810 --> 00:27:50.390
then this is kind of
useless, and it would break.

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:52.940
So the better way to do this
is through a persistent volume

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:54.030
claim.

00:27:54.030 --> 00:27:58.160
A persistent volume claim
is basically a contract,

00:27:58.160 --> 00:28:01.160
and that contract
doesn't name in the pod

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:02.960
spec a specific volume.

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:07.060
Instead, it has a claim name.

00:28:07.060 --> 00:28:09.550
So this abstraction then
allows to Kubernetes

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:13.150
to bind the PVC claim to
the persistent volume.

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:16.522
And then when the pod comes
up and it requests the volume,

00:28:16.522 --> 00:28:17.605
the volume can be created.

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:24.390
And this is an example
of what the PVC looks

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:25.680
like, the claim looks like.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:29.140
In that claim you specify
what type of storage you want.

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:31.830
So in this case, we've said
it's a 100 gigs of stores that I

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:33.660
want, so the characteristics.

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:37.540
And then another abstraction,
which is the storage class.

00:28:37.540 --> 00:28:41.050
The storage class can be
set by your cluster admin.

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:44.050
If you're in a company that
has a storage or cluster admin,

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:46.720
and they are choosing
what type of storage

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:49.270
should be available to
what type of applications,

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:52.420
they may create a storage
class, say a fast storage class

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:53.980
that refers to as SSDs.

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:57.130
Or a slow storage class that
refers to a specific type

00:28:57.130 --> 00:29:00.270
of disk, in a specific cloud.

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:04.740
This separation of concerns
allows for portability,

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:09.350
and allows for policy
setting around storage.

00:29:09.350 --> 00:29:12.690
But the real beauty of
this is, that it's actually

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:14.290
dynamically provisioned.

00:29:14.290 --> 00:29:17.520
So, when you instantiate
or create your pod,

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:20.910
the PVC gets created,
and it's at that time

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:23.880
that Kubernetes will go ahead
and dynamically provision

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:24.690
the storage.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:28.140
So it doesn't require that the
storage is pre-provisioned.

00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:30.060
There's no wastage here.

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:35.310
Also, you would set the
policy in your default storage

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:38.490
provisioner, that when
the PVC goes away,

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:39.820
the storage goes away.

00:29:39.820 --> 00:29:42.990
So it also cleans
up after itself.

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:45.750
That's one of the nice
things about dynamic storage

00:29:45.750 --> 00:29:46.776
provisioning.

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:54.930
One thing I want to point out
is that in our next release,

00:29:54.930 --> 00:29:57.330
while this is a nice set
of abstractions you've got,

00:29:57.330 --> 00:30:00.540
storage provider, you've got
the user with the PVC claim,

00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:02.130
and you've got a cluster admin.

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:05.870
Not everyone needs this
love of separation.

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:09.390
And Kubernetes does a nice job
in our next release of just

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:12.300
picking sane defaults for you.

00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:16.180
So, pre-installed in the
next version of Kubernetes

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:19.620
and Container Engine, there are
these default storage classes.

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:22.530
In Google Cloud, it'll
be a GCE persistent disk,

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:25.290
in Amazon and OpenStack,
these will be the defaults.

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:30.390
And so you don't have to, in
fact, even say in your PVC

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:31.930
that there's a storage class.

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:33.930
This is the default class,
that will be assumed.

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:37.860
And when the PVC gets
provisioned, gets created,

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.910
the storage automatically
gets provisioned.

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:43.790
So I'd like to actually
demonstrate this.

00:30:43.790 --> 00:30:47.270
I think the most power of
dynamic storage provisioning

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:50.990
comes in when you use it
with a Stateful application.

00:30:50.990 --> 00:30:53.300
And I think, if
Kelsey is here, we

00:30:53.300 --> 00:30:56.030
might actually have a
Stateful application

00:30:56.030 --> 00:31:01.850
that can be used to demonstrate
dynamic storage provisioning.

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:03.380
KELSEY: You like
live demos, right?

00:31:05.986 --> 00:31:08.110
All right, so I don't have
any slides for you guys.

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:10.540
So I apologize.

00:31:10.540 --> 00:31:13.270
And we're going to talk about
this dynamic provisioning,

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:16.100
but provisioning storage without
an application to use it,

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:17.470
is kind of pointless.

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:20.162
So we're going to deploy
an app called Consul.

00:31:20.162 --> 00:31:21.870
So if you've never
seen Consul, it's just

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:24.910
a key value store that
has built in replication,

00:31:24.910 --> 00:31:27.270
and built in high
availability, using Wrapped,

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:30.190
and it also has a side
communication channel

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:33.010
using a swim protocol, so that
members can find each other,

00:31:33.010 --> 00:31:35.180
even in the case of failures.

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:37.060
So the recommendation
for a Consul,

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:38.740
is that you run three nodes.

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:42.340
Now, the typical deployment is
each node has its own volume,

00:31:42.340 --> 00:31:44.620
and is responsible
for its own data set,

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:47.170
and will replicate between
each of the members.

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:48.940
So we want to deploy
this and Kubernetes.

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:50.815
Now, if you're doing
this for the first time,

00:31:50.815 --> 00:31:53.023
you don't want to learn a
particular cloud provider's

00:31:53.023 --> 00:31:55.120
way of provisioning
storage, and making sure

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:58.892
that it's attached to the
proper node in the cluster.

00:31:58.892 --> 00:32:00.850
So what we're going to
do is, work through this

00:32:00.850 --> 00:32:02.530
and see how you do
it in Kubernetes.

00:32:02.530 --> 00:32:04.738
So the first thing that
we're going to take a look at

00:32:04.738 --> 00:32:07.810
is my current set up, and
my Google Cloud project.

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:14.310
I'm going to show you the
disks, a compute disk list.

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:19.310
So here you'll see that
I can't spell at all.

00:32:19.310 --> 00:32:20.900
You just got to roll with it.

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:22.730
So here are the disk
that I currently have,

00:32:22.730 --> 00:32:24.690
and those are attached
to my virtual machines.

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:25.880
So when you think
about this concept,

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:27.546
you already see it
for virtual machines.

00:32:27.546 --> 00:32:30.260
When you click on a VM, no
one tells you to go provision

00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:32.180
a disk first to attach that VM.

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:33.920
You assume that the
VM needs storage,

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:36.090
and that cloud provider
should create that for you.

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:38.330
We're taking that
and then extending it

00:32:38.330 --> 00:32:39.790
to the application level.

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:41.665
So this is how I want
you to think about this

00:32:41.665 --> 00:32:42.930
when you see this in action.

00:32:42.930 --> 00:32:45.770
So now we need to describe
our StatefulSet So

00:32:45.770 --> 00:32:48.020
what are we to do here,
there's a lot of content here,

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:50.030
but I'm going to highlight
the important parts.

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:51.770
So here, I'm saying
that I want three

00:32:51.770 --> 00:32:53.700
copies of this application.

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:55.400
So, what will happen
here is Kubernetes

00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:58.434
will create node zero,
node one, and node two.

00:32:58.434 --> 00:33:01.100
And the next thing we need to do
is have storage assigned to us.

00:33:01.100 --> 00:33:03.433
So we're going to scroll all
the way down to the bottom,

00:33:03.433 --> 00:33:05.420
and we're going to
highlight this volume claim.

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:07.250
Now here, I am using a template.

00:33:07.250 --> 00:33:08.510
Now the reason why I'm
using a template here,

00:33:08.510 --> 00:33:10.218
I'm going to need
Kubernetes to stamp out

00:33:10.218 --> 00:33:11.630
three versions of this.

00:33:11.630 --> 00:33:14.480
I'm going to have a request,
based on the documentation,

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:17.240
I need about 10 gigs of
storage per application.

00:33:17.240 --> 00:33:19.404
Consul isn't designed
to hold a lot of data,

00:33:19.404 --> 00:33:21.320
it's just there for your
cluster configuration

00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:22.609
and you're monitoring bits.

00:33:22.609 --> 00:33:24.900
And also we don't really care
what the storage classes.

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:27.230
So if I was running this in
a different cloud provider,

00:33:27.230 --> 00:33:29.690
ideally, the cluster
running in that provider,

00:33:29.690 --> 00:33:32.520
would do the automated
storage provision for me.

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:35.070
So, what we want to do now is
submit this to our cluster.

00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:36.410
Now we have some other
bits that are there,

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:37.660
but we won't go through those.

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:40.055
Things like configuration
files, and TLS certificates

00:33:40.055 --> 00:33:42.230
so we can have a
secure console cluster,

00:33:42.230 --> 00:33:44.220
but here want to
focus on the storage.

00:33:44.220 --> 00:33:47.660
So, we're going to push this
to our Kubernetes master,

00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:49.655
and while we do this,
let's look and see

00:33:49.655 --> 00:33:51.530
what happens to the disk
while this is going.

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:54.027
So, we'll just
throw a watch there.

00:33:54.027 --> 00:33:56.110
So, we see that we only
have three disks, so we'll

00:33:56.110 --> 00:33:57.910
push this to Kubernetes now.

00:33:57.910 --> 00:34:03.430
So kubectl create dash
f statefulsets console.

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:07.210
So this gets submitted to the
cluster, and you see instantly,

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:09.190
it goes out and
provisions the storage

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:11.309
based on the needs
of the operator.

00:34:11.309 --> 00:34:12.850
So now at this point,
we can actually

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:15.320
look at a few pods
being created.

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:18.377
So, each of our applications
comes up one at a time.

00:34:18.377 --> 00:34:19.960
And most of these
kind of applications

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:21.480
need to come up in
a specific order,

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:23.230
mainly because they
need to form a quorum,

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:25.150
and there's some
bootstrapping phase.

00:34:25.150 --> 00:34:27.310
So node one is coming up.

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:29.380
That's going to be
scheduled by the scheduler

00:34:29.380 --> 00:34:30.820
to a particular machine.

00:34:30.820 --> 00:34:32.739
Once that decision
is made, that volume

00:34:32.739 --> 00:34:34.659
will be then attached
to that machine

00:34:34.659 --> 00:34:37.510
and injected into the
container at runtime.

00:34:37.510 --> 00:34:39.760
Now, we'll do this a couple
more times until on behalf

00:34:39.760 --> 00:34:42.530
of the entire cluster
up and running.

00:34:42.530 --> 00:34:44.823
So let's give that a few
seconds and see where we are.

00:34:44.823 --> 00:34:45.989
So we have two out of three.

00:34:48.364 --> 00:34:49.280
Let's do a watch here.

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:53.300
All right, so this is creating.

00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:56.210
Now as this creates, we're going
to actually go ahead and try

00:34:56.210 --> 00:34:59.850
to communicate with our
cluster to see where we are.

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:03.035
So, here we are going to do,
is do a bit of port forwarding.

00:35:03.035 --> 00:35:05.160
I don't want you guys
interacting with my clusters,

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:08.480
so we're going to do this
over a private channel.

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:10.280
So once I have this
connection in place,

00:35:10.280 --> 00:35:12.363
I can start to interact
with this console cluster.

00:35:12.363 --> 00:35:16.590
I can run things
like Consul Consul,

00:35:16.590 --> 00:35:21.200
come on, Consul, members.

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:23.600
And you notice that there's
only one member listed here.

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:26.180
This is because they don't
know anything about each other.

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:28.805
They've all coming up with their
own independent stores volume,

00:35:28.805 --> 00:35:30.890
and they do not have
the cluster membership

00:35:30.890 --> 00:35:32.750
configuration stored yet.

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:34.739
We need to join the cluster.

00:35:34.739 --> 00:35:37.280
So, what I'm going to do here,
you've got to raise your hand.

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:38.480
OK, yes.

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:42.137
AUDIENCE: Can you show the
storage that [INAUDIBLE]?

00:35:42.137 --> 00:35:43.220
KELSEY: Ah, good question.

00:35:43.220 --> 00:35:47.062
So in Kubernetes, we
talked about these volumes.

00:35:47.062 --> 00:35:49.270
So, in Kubernetes, they're
called persistent volumes.

00:35:49.270 --> 00:35:52.060
So if we say kubectl
get pv, for short,

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:54.370
we can actually see
those three volumes.

00:35:54.370 --> 00:35:56.620
And you'll notice that they're
named some random name,

00:35:56.620 --> 00:35:58.930
but they're also
attached to a claim.

00:35:58.930 --> 00:36:02.050
So we list up the claim,
that PVC, what you'll see

00:36:02.050 --> 00:36:03.550
is this convention.

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:05.480
You'll see the data
directories, the volume

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:07.360
that I'm specifying
inside of my manifest

00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:09.040
and where I would
like it to be mounted,

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:11.770
and also the name of the
pod that it belongs to.

00:36:11.770 --> 00:36:14.500
So StatefulSets gives yours
pods a consistent name,

00:36:14.500 --> 00:36:16.840
consoles zero, console
one, console two.

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:18.580
Now this is cool if
you go up to five,

00:36:18.580 --> 00:36:20.180
and you have to scale back down.

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:22.870
We can configure our storage
not to be deleted when our nodes

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:25.690
or pods go away, so that
way if we scale back up,

00:36:25.690 --> 00:36:29.770
you're guaranteed to get the
same disk that you had before,

00:36:29.770 --> 00:36:31.090
attached to the same name.

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:33.597
So you don't have to go around
and worry about that, OK?

00:36:33.597 --> 00:36:35.680
So this is what's happening
underneath the scenes.

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:37.630
Everything is bound
to the right pods,

00:36:37.630 --> 00:36:39.338
and that will stay
this way and we'll see

00:36:39.338 --> 00:36:41.600
what happens if nodes go away.

00:36:41.600 --> 00:36:44.460
All right, so given that,
what we want to do now

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:47.022
is see what we can do about
this cluster membership.

00:36:47.022 --> 00:36:48.480
So we have council
members running,

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:50.310
but we only have one member.

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:53.780
So what we need to do
next, is form the cluster.

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:55.820
Then what we're going
to do is use a job here.

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:59.160
So since we have consistent
naming, using StatefulSets,

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:02.040
notice here that can
reference all of my members

00:37:02.040 --> 00:37:03.270
by their DNS name.

00:37:03.270 --> 00:37:06.160
Now, regardless of the IP
address, this will resolve.

00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:09.000
So, I'm just going to use
the same console container,

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:12.720
it's going to run the console
a join command, using all three

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:14.192
members of that cluster.

00:37:14.192 --> 00:37:16.150
So let's run this job
now and see what happens.

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:20.269
Kubectl create dash f jobs.

00:37:20.269 --> 00:37:21.810
So we're looking at
our members here.

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:22.610
We'll run the job.

00:37:25.410 --> 00:37:27.985
So this will go out and
communicate to all the members.

00:37:27.985 --> 00:37:29.610
So if all of this is
working, we should

00:37:29.610 --> 00:37:33.280
be able to see the
console UI here.

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:35.410
So let's check this out.

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:38.410
So we see all three members,
all help checks are passing.

00:37:38.410 --> 00:37:41.530
Now why is this volume
claim necessary?

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:44.390
Why do we need this association
to this particular pod

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:45.970
name and its storage?

00:37:45.970 --> 00:37:49.250
Well, what happens if one
of the pods goes away?

00:37:49.250 --> 00:37:52.330
So here what I'm going to do
is delete one of our members.

00:37:52.330 --> 00:37:56.620
Kubectl, delete pods, and
we'll give it this name.

00:37:56.620 --> 00:37:58.980
Now notice the IP address
of this particular pod.

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:01.420
The IP address here is 0.11.

00:38:01.420 --> 00:38:03.730
That's how all the other
members know it is as,

00:38:03.730 --> 00:38:05.290
and that particular
member has inside

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:09.190
of its stores directory, the IP
addresses of its other peers.

00:38:09.190 --> 00:38:11.279
So, if it goes
away, the only way

00:38:11.279 --> 00:38:13.570
it's going to be able to send
signals back to its peers

00:38:13.570 --> 00:38:16.240
that it has a different IP
address, if it comes back

00:38:16.240 --> 00:38:17.677
with the exact same volume.

00:38:17.677 --> 00:38:19.510
So it's critical that
we get the same volume

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:20.810
if something happens.

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:23.020
So we'll delete it here.

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:25.540
And at this point, the
heartbeats will timeout.

00:38:25.540 --> 00:38:28.820
And this thing should
go from alive to failed.

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:30.070
And let's see if that happens.

00:38:30.070 --> 00:38:31.690
So now it's failed.

00:38:31.690 --> 00:38:33.356
Now, what we expect
Kubernetes to do

00:38:33.356 --> 00:38:35.230
is automatically replace
this because we said

00:38:35.230 --> 00:38:36.680
that we want three of these.

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:39.100
So if we look at pods, we see
that it's automatically been

00:38:39.100 --> 00:38:41.740
recreated, just 15 seconds ago.

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:45.347
And now the storage is being
remounted to the new machine.

00:38:45.347 --> 00:38:46.930
So what should happen
is, this machine

00:38:46.930 --> 00:38:49.512
should come up, grab
its data volume,

00:38:49.512 --> 00:38:51.220
have information about
the other members,

00:38:51.220 --> 00:38:54.080
and start to broadcast signals
about its new IP address

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:55.425
if it has changed.

00:38:55.425 --> 00:38:56.800
So it will give
us a few moments,

00:38:56.800 --> 00:38:59.662
and see if we can actually
have our cluster come back

00:38:59.662 --> 00:39:01.120
and have a full
quorum ready to go.

00:39:03.841 --> 00:39:05.590
Well the demo guys are
like, I don't know.

00:39:11.973 --> 00:39:13.420
Tick, tock tick.

00:39:13.420 --> 00:39:15.170
Some people are on the
edge of their seats

00:39:15.170 --> 00:39:17.432
like, it ain't gonna work.

00:39:17.432 --> 00:39:20.550
I have confidence.

00:39:20.550 --> 00:39:21.620
I think I do.

00:39:21.620 --> 00:39:22.950
And it's back up and running.

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:25.320
That's how you use dynamic
provisioning with Kubernetes

00:39:25.320 --> 00:39:25.920
1.6.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:26.419
Thank you.

00:39:34.136 --> 00:39:35.260
APARNA: Over to the slides.

00:39:35.260 --> 00:39:36.330
Thank you, Kelsey.

00:39:36.330 --> 00:39:38.250
So you saw dynamic
provisioning, didn't

00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:40.000
need to have the storage
there beforehand,

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:43.827
and then the storage
persists afterwards.

00:39:43.827 --> 00:39:46.160
The next feature, or the last
set of features, actually,

00:39:46.160 --> 00:39:48.940
that we're going to preview
are related to scheduling.

00:39:48.940 --> 00:39:52.040
There's a number of
advanced scheduling features

00:39:52.040 --> 00:39:53.780
that are coming in
the next release,

00:39:53.780 --> 00:39:57.080
and these should help
as you're provisioning

00:39:57.080 --> 00:40:00.770
larger clusters and provisioning
clusters across zones.

00:40:00.770 --> 00:40:03.080
So, first of all,
we do recommend

00:40:03.080 --> 00:40:06.230
that clusters should be
provisioned across zones

00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:08.480
because there is a
likelihood, or possibility,

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:10.430
of a single zone going down.

00:40:10.430 --> 00:40:15.431
It's fairly easy to provision
a cluster across the zones.

00:40:15.431 --> 00:40:17.430
Essentially, when you
create the cluster, or you

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:21.320
specify that you'd
like additional zones,

00:40:21.320 --> 00:40:24.380
and it'll create nodes in
those additional zones.

00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:27.260
And by default, the
scheduler will spread prods

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:29.840
across all the available
zones in a cluster.

00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:30.456
That's nice.

00:40:30.456 --> 00:40:32.580
You can also do it once
you've created the cluster,

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:35.220
and you want to edit it and add
zones, you can also do that.

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:38.540
That's nice, but there
are certain things

00:40:38.540 --> 00:40:41.060
when you're working with a
cluster that spans zones,

00:40:41.060 --> 00:40:43.410
that you might want to avoid.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:47.300
And that's where some of
the custom of scheduling

00:40:47.300 --> 00:40:50.940
and advance scheduling
techniques come in handy.

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:53.600
One of these techniques
is called node affinity.

00:40:53.600 --> 00:40:56.720
Node affinity is where a
pod expresses a preference

00:40:56.720 --> 00:40:58.400
for a certain node.

00:40:58.400 --> 00:41:01.340
And it could be two types,
it could be a preference,

00:41:01.340 --> 00:41:03.560
or it could be a requirement.

00:41:03.560 --> 00:41:07.820
The node itself, the
characteristics of the node,

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:09.170
be specified in the pod spec.

00:41:09.170 --> 00:41:11.480
So this is an
example of a pod spec

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:13.370
that is defining in an affinity.

00:41:13.370 --> 00:41:16.280
It is a node affinity
that says that it prefers

00:41:16.280 --> 00:41:20.240
a node that is in a
particular region,

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:22.910
in this case its us-central1-b.

00:41:22.910 --> 00:41:30.350
And that's done through defining
an operator that operates

00:41:30.350 --> 00:41:32.460
and connects a key to a value.

00:41:32.460 --> 00:41:36.470
The key, in this case, is the
zone of the node, and the value

00:41:36.470 --> 00:41:39.710
here that we're looking
for is us-central1-b,

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:45.440
and the operator is in, which
is I want the zone to be

00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:47.360
in us-central1-b.

00:41:47.360 --> 00:41:51.050
There are other operators, so
it's kind of like an equation

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:53.810
that you can set up, or
a regular expression.

00:41:53.810 --> 00:41:56.780
Other operators that are
supported are in, or not in,

00:41:56.780 --> 00:42:00.710
you could say not in this
zone, or such and such exists,

00:42:00.710 --> 00:42:03.170
resource exists, or does not
exist, or is greater than,

00:42:03.170 --> 00:42:04.100
or less than.

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:06.860
Those are the operators
that are supported.

00:42:06.860 --> 00:42:10.310
So what will happen here is
that, since we've expressed

00:42:10.310 --> 00:42:12.470
a preference and
not a requirement,

00:42:12.470 --> 00:42:18.050
when the pod labeled App1, comes
in and has to be scheduled,

00:42:18.050 --> 00:42:20.990
it will have a preference to
be scheduled in us-central1-b

00:42:20.990 --> 00:42:23.660
and we have three nodes
in our multi-zone cluster

00:42:23.660 --> 00:42:25.347
that are in us-central1-b,
so if there's

00:42:25.347 --> 00:42:26.930
space available on
any of those, it'll

00:42:26.930 --> 00:42:28.880
get preferentially
scheduled there.

00:42:28.880 --> 00:42:31.190
It can still get scheduled
in the other zones

00:42:31.190 --> 00:42:33.620
if there is not space
available, or you

00:42:33.620 --> 00:42:36.290
can make this a hard
requirement if you

00:42:36.290 --> 00:42:37.940
prefer you to use required.

00:42:37.940 --> 00:42:39.710
So that's node affinity.

00:42:39.710 --> 00:42:43.670
There are a number of built
keys for nodes already.

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:46.130
These are the built keys,
so it could be a zone,

00:42:46.130 --> 00:42:48.240
it could be region, it
could be the host name.

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:50.090
So you could say, I
want a particular VM

00:42:50.090 --> 00:42:53.837
and that's where it should be,
or a particular type of VM.

00:42:53.837 --> 00:42:55.670
It could be a preemptible
VM, you could say,

00:42:55.670 --> 00:42:59.150
I want these applications only
to be run on preemptible VMs,

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:02.720
and you specify that here,
and other things like

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:06.790
the type of disk, whether
it's SSD, what type of OS.

00:43:06.790 --> 00:43:09.200
But the best thing is that
you can also write your own.

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:13.220
You can write your own,
essentially labels, on nodes

00:43:13.220 --> 00:43:15.050
and then tell the
scheduler, I want

00:43:15.050 --> 00:43:17.710
to schedule these pods on a
node that has these labels.

00:43:20.330 --> 00:43:21.880
That's node affinity.

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:25.340
A related feature is
inter-pod affinity.

00:43:25.340 --> 00:43:28.790
So now, you want to specify
where a particular application

00:43:28.790 --> 00:43:31.790
should be scheduled, given
where a certain other service is

00:43:31.790 --> 00:43:32.870
scheduled.

00:43:32.870 --> 00:43:34.710
This can be very useful.

00:43:34.710 --> 00:43:37.490
So again, we're talking
about multi-zone clusters

00:43:37.490 --> 00:43:38.790
as an example.

00:43:38.790 --> 00:43:40.889
If you have two
services that need

00:43:40.889 --> 00:43:42.680
to communicate with
each other, say there's

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:47.072
an east-west pattern, and you
don't want those two services

00:43:47.072 --> 00:43:48.530
to be scheduled in
different zones,

00:43:48.530 --> 00:43:51.440
because then there's egress
charges associated with that.

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:54.670
Actually the latency is
very low in Google Cloud,

00:43:54.670 --> 00:43:57.630
but there are egress charges
that you may want to avoid.

00:43:57.630 --> 00:44:00.530
So for ensuring east-west
communication within zone,

00:44:00.530 --> 00:44:02.990
you can set the
inter-pod affinity.

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:04.440
And this is an example of that.

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:08.210
So this is a replica set,
and there are three replicas,

00:44:08.210 --> 00:44:10.520
and the name is App2.

00:44:10.520 --> 00:44:13.520
And what we would like,
is for this App2 pod

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:17.690
to have an affinity to App1,
to wherever the zone is where

00:44:17.690 --> 00:44:21.210
App1 is scheduled, we want App2
to be scheduled there as well.

00:44:21.210 --> 00:44:23.390
And in this case, we're not
expressing a preference,

00:44:23.390 --> 00:44:24.740
we're saying that it's required.

00:44:24.740 --> 00:44:29.570
So we're say here, pod affinity,
required, and the operator

00:44:29.570 --> 00:44:33.120
again, is in, and the value
is App1, and the key is zone.

00:44:33.120 --> 00:44:36.620
So now we want App2 to be
co-scheduled in the same zone,

00:44:36.620 --> 00:44:41.150
so that east-west communication
is less expensive.

00:44:41.150 --> 00:44:43.670
There are other use
cases, of course.

00:44:43.670 --> 00:44:48.620
It also works if you want to
co-locate north-south services,

00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:50.730
and then there's
also anti-affinity.

00:44:50.730 --> 00:44:53.360
So affinity is when you want to
pods to be scheduled together,

00:44:53.360 --> 00:44:56.900
anti-affinity is when you
want to spread pods across,

00:44:56.900 --> 00:45:01.967
so you want to spread your
services across your cluster.

00:45:01.967 --> 00:45:04.300
This could be because you
will have some antagonist pods

00:45:04.300 --> 00:45:06.880
that you want to keep
separated, or you

00:45:06.880 --> 00:45:09.740
want sole tendency, or a
variety of other use cases.

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:14.140
So this is the other advanced
scheduling capability

00:45:14.140 --> 00:45:17.050
that's going to be
available in this release.

00:45:17.050 --> 00:45:19.060
And the third one,
which I think is perhaps

00:45:19.060 --> 00:45:20.950
the most interesting
one, is this concept

00:45:20.950 --> 00:45:23.950
of taints and tolerations.

00:45:23.950 --> 00:45:26.380
And this is where
it's not really

00:45:26.380 --> 00:45:29.800
a single pod, or a
single set of services,

00:45:29.800 --> 00:45:31.960
that you want to set
rules on, but a whole

00:45:31.960 --> 00:45:36.040
set of services that should
avoid a particular node.

00:45:36.040 --> 00:45:38.560
Then what you do is
you taint the node.

00:45:38.560 --> 00:45:40.660
You put a taint on
the node, and then you

00:45:40.660 --> 00:45:43.300
put a toleration on
specific pods that

00:45:43.300 --> 00:45:45.610
should tolerate that taint.

00:45:45.610 --> 00:45:53.100
So an example is, here we
have a node that has two GPUs

00:45:53.100 --> 00:45:54.940
whereas the other
nodes do not have GPUs.

00:45:54.940 --> 00:45:57.990
So we can put a taint
on that node that says--

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:01.290
and that's how you do it,
kubectl, taint node, GPU true--

00:46:01.290 --> 00:46:03.180
and so it prefers no schedule.

00:46:03.180 --> 00:46:06.510
So do not schedule on this
one, because it is a GPU node

00:46:06.510 --> 00:46:10.050
and it's maybe more
expensive, or is special.

00:46:10.050 --> 00:46:12.150
And then that the
application, or the pod,

00:46:12.150 --> 00:46:15.450
that actually requires GPUs,
you would put a toleration

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:18.630
on that one that
says the key is GPU,

00:46:18.630 --> 00:46:21.900
and I have a
toleration for that.

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:25.320
So when that pod comes around,
it'll be scheduled to node one,

00:46:25.320 --> 00:46:28.000
but every other pod will
avoid that tainted node.

00:46:32.010 --> 00:46:34.350
This is very similar
to affinity in

00:46:34.350 --> 00:46:36.690
that you can use required,
or preferred, and so forth.

00:46:36.690 --> 00:46:41.460
But where this is really
interesting is in a feature

00:46:41.460 --> 00:46:44.310
that we call
forgiveness, where you

00:46:44.310 --> 00:46:51.600
put a taint on every node that
says you should stop and evict

00:46:51.600 --> 00:46:54.330
all running pods,
in the case of say,

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:57.750
node becoming unresponsive,
or disk not being available,

00:46:57.750 --> 00:46:59.160
in the case of a failure.

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:02.040
If the node fails, stop
the pods and evict them.

00:47:02.040 --> 00:47:03.420
And then you can
put a toleration

00:47:03.420 --> 00:47:05.190
on the pods
themselves that says,

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:08.880
how long should this pod remain
on this unresponsive node?

00:47:08.880 --> 00:47:10.720
When should it get rescheduled?

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:14.280
So this is a really nice
feature that actually recently

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:19.170
had a very good use case, when
there was an S3 outage on AWS

00:47:19.170 --> 00:47:23.310
last week, there's a company,
I think they're called--

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:24.920
I always forget their name--

00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:28.220
I think they called Strype, or--

00:47:28.220 --> 00:47:28.880
anyway.

00:47:28.880 --> 00:47:32.070
There's a company that
was using this feature,

00:47:32.070 --> 00:47:34.770
and when their nodes
became unresponsive,

00:47:34.770 --> 00:47:37.152
their pods automatically
got rescheduled.

00:47:37.152 --> 00:47:39.110
Because the disk wasn't
available on that node,

00:47:39.110 --> 00:47:41.790
their pods had this toleration,
and after a certain amount

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:44.880
of time, they automatically got
scheduled to a node that did

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:49.710
have space available.

00:47:49.710 --> 00:47:51.860
So those are some of
the advanced features

00:47:51.860 --> 00:47:53.580
that are coming.

00:47:53.580 --> 00:47:56.450
The last, and not least,
one is the ability

00:47:56.450 --> 00:47:58.550
to set up a custom scheduler.

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:00.590
So you know this gives
you, all this gives

00:48:00.590 --> 00:48:04.940
you, a lot of flexibility to
deploy the way you want to,

00:48:04.940 --> 00:48:06.980
but you're not limited
by the default scheduler.

00:48:06.980 --> 00:48:09.290
You can actually bring
in your own scheduler,

00:48:09.290 --> 00:48:11.900
and extend Kubernetes
to use whatever

00:48:11.900 --> 00:48:14.510
rules you want to have.

00:48:14.510 --> 00:48:16.850
Let's see maybe Kelsey has
a demo left of the custom

00:48:16.850 --> 00:48:19.644
scheduler as well.

00:48:19.644 --> 00:48:23.470
KELSEY: Oh, yeah, I
have a custom scheduler.

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:25.790
All right, so our I kind of
talked about this ability

00:48:25.790 --> 00:48:28.430
to give your nodes
attributes, ad-hoc attributes,

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:30.080
we call them annotations.

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:31.990
So on each node, if
you look at them,

00:48:31.990 --> 00:48:33.950
if you were to list our
nodes in this cluster,

00:48:33.950 --> 00:48:35.479
have three nodes in our cluster.

00:48:35.479 --> 00:48:37.520
And what we can do, is
get a little bit more info

00:48:37.520 --> 00:48:38.019
about them.

00:48:38.019 --> 00:48:40.520
So if I say dash
0 equals yaml, we

00:48:40.520 --> 00:48:42.740
get a bit more data
about these nodes.

00:48:42.740 --> 00:48:45.620
And here, we can actually see
different attributes, labels,

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:47.400
annotations, and so forth.

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:50.480
Now, given that, we have
things like region and zone.

00:48:50.480 --> 00:48:52.190
I want to build a
custom scheduler that

00:48:52.190 --> 00:48:55.140
lets me choose the node
based on the cheapest price.

00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:55.640
OK?

00:48:55.640 --> 00:48:57.806
So all of these notes may
have different attributes,

00:48:57.806 --> 00:49:00.620
but, hey, what I want to do, is
favor the cheapest nodes first,

00:49:00.620 --> 00:49:03.090
before moving on
to the next node.

00:49:03.090 --> 00:49:05.950
So one thing we need to do is
annotate our particular nodes.

00:49:05.950 --> 00:49:07.700
So I have a little
tool, called annotator,

00:49:07.700 --> 00:49:09.510
that just goes out
to the company's API,

00:49:09.510 --> 00:49:12.270
and writes annotations on
them based on the price.

00:49:12.270 --> 00:49:15.030
So we'll run this, and we see
that one of the nodes is $0.10

00:49:15.030 --> 00:49:18.240
per hour, $0.80 per
hour, $0.40 per hour.

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:20.460
So ideally any workload
that I execute,

00:49:20.460 --> 00:49:24.320
I want to run on ab, or 8b, 1b.

00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:24.890
OK?

00:49:24.890 --> 00:49:26.100
That's the goal.

00:49:26.100 --> 00:49:28.290
So what is it required
to actually run

00:49:28.290 --> 00:49:30.300
multiple schedulers
in Kubernetes?

00:49:30.300 --> 00:49:32.697
Well you need to do a little
bit of things differently.

00:49:32.697 --> 00:49:34.530
So here, I want to take
a deployment object,

00:49:34.530 --> 00:49:36.547
so these deployments run
inside of Kubernetes,

00:49:36.547 --> 00:49:38.130
and but default, the
default scheduler

00:49:38.130 --> 00:49:41.040
pulls them, and runs
them inside your cluster.

00:49:41.040 --> 00:49:44.800
Now, let's say you want to
use a different scheduler.

00:49:44.800 --> 00:49:46.940
Here is the annotation
on my workload,

00:49:46.940 --> 00:49:49.270
in this case of
deployment, and here I'm

00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:51.370
using a custom
scheduler by name.

00:49:51.370 --> 00:49:52.885
Now normally, this
would be default.

00:49:52.885 --> 00:49:54.760
But when you get to this
level of Kubernetes,

00:49:54.760 --> 00:49:57.640
you get to have a
scheduler named after you,

00:49:57.640 --> 00:49:58.880
if you want that.

00:49:58.880 --> 00:50:00.550
So this is the
Hightower scheduler.

00:50:00.550 --> 00:50:03.730
And the Hightower scheduler
will pick nodes based on price.

00:50:03.730 --> 00:50:05.860
So when this workload
gets executed,

00:50:05.860 --> 00:50:08.110
it will be ignored by
the default scheduler.

00:50:08.110 --> 00:50:09.100
That's the key here.

00:50:09.100 --> 00:50:12.954
So these schedules get to run in
parallel inside of Kubernetes.

00:50:12.954 --> 00:50:14.370
So I'm going to
run this workload,

00:50:14.370 --> 00:50:16.720
and you notice here that
we do have CPU and memory

00:50:16.720 --> 00:50:17.750
requirements.

00:50:17.750 --> 00:50:21.280
So those two predicates can't
be ignored just for the cheapest

00:50:21.280 --> 00:50:22.920
price, you need to
run on the node that

00:50:22.920 --> 00:50:25.210
also fits our workload.

00:50:25.210 --> 00:50:31.532
So what I'll do here, is deploy
this workload, Nginx custom.

00:50:31.532 --> 00:50:33.490
So at this point, we now
have a pod of running.

00:50:33.490 --> 00:50:38.080
And so we'll actually show
that here, kubectl, get pods.

00:50:38.080 --> 00:50:40.080
And we'll throw a watch there.

00:50:40.080 --> 00:50:42.550
So notice here that it's
sitting there pending.

00:50:42.550 --> 00:50:43.720
How long will this pend?

00:50:43.720 --> 00:50:45.640
This will pend
forever, actually.

00:50:45.640 --> 00:50:48.220
Because there is no scheduler
running inside of the cluster

00:50:48.220 --> 00:50:49.930
that knows what to do with this.

00:50:49.930 --> 00:50:52.599
It's being ignored by
the default scheduler.

00:50:52.599 --> 00:50:54.140
Only thing that we
have at this point

00:50:54.140 --> 00:50:56.473
is our desire state that it
should be running somewhere,

00:50:56.473 --> 00:50:58.850
but there is no scheduler
that's in charge of this.

00:50:58.850 --> 00:51:00.131
So I wrote my own scheduler.

00:51:00.131 --> 00:51:02.130
It's on my laptop, it's
also available on GitHub

00:51:02.130 --> 00:51:03.620
if you want to
check out the code.

00:51:03.620 --> 00:51:06.780
So here I'm going to
build a scheduler.

00:51:06.780 --> 00:51:10.690
And I'm one to now have my
scheduler point at my cluster,

00:51:10.690 --> 00:51:12.610
and schedule this
particular workload.

00:51:12.610 --> 00:51:15.560
So here we'll just run it.

00:51:15.560 --> 00:51:18.000
So we're starting our scheduler.

00:51:18.000 --> 00:51:21.390
And notice here that we
scheduled this particular pod,

00:51:21.390 --> 00:51:22.440
to this particular node.

00:51:22.440 --> 00:51:24.815
And I want to remind you guys
of the price of these nodes

00:51:24.815 --> 00:51:26.910
is to make sure that we
landed in the right place.

00:51:26.910 --> 00:51:29.940
So we just do
annotator dash L again,

00:51:29.940 --> 00:51:32.130
and we notice that this
was the cheapest price.

00:51:32.130 --> 00:51:34.170
So this is where we
expected it to land,

00:51:34.170 --> 00:51:35.920
and there we go,
it's up and running.

00:51:35.920 --> 00:51:37.410
Now, if I were to
scale this out,

00:51:37.410 --> 00:51:38.850
I expect the same behavior.

00:51:38.850 --> 00:51:41.730
I prefer the cheapest node
until your workload no longer

00:51:41.730 --> 00:51:44.490
fits before you spillover
to the next node.

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:47.460
So let's scale this up a little
bit to see how that works.

00:51:47.460 --> 00:51:50.010
So what we'll do is
say kubectl scale,

00:51:50.010 --> 00:51:54.720
deployments, and this is
going to be Nginx custom,

00:51:54.720 --> 00:51:57.110
and then we're going
to give a replica set.

00:51:57.110 --> 00:51:58.720
What number should we go up to?

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:00.480
AUDIENCE: 42.

00:52:00.480 --> 00:52:01.650
KELSEY: 42?

00:52:01.650 --> 00:52:05.560
Round it down to 40, divide by
4 is about 10, so we'll do that.

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:11.530
So we have 10 of these
things right now.

00:52:11.530 --> 00:52:13.230
And we'll see what
our scheduler does,

00:52:13.230 --> 00:52:16.690
so it's continuously
preferring the cheaper node.

00:52:16.690 --> 00:52:18.667
Now eventually, that
node will no longer

00:52:18.667 --> 00:52:20.500
be able to host our
workload, so we may have

00:52:20.500 --> 00:52:22.570
to spill over to the next node.

00:52:22.570 --> 00:52:26.054
Ideally, we want to spill
over to the cheaper node.

00:52:26.054 --> 00:52:27.470
So there we go,
it's automatically

00:52:27.470 --> 00:52:28.732
moved onto the cheapest node.

00:52:28.732 --> 00:52:30.440
So my scheduler is
doing the right thing.

00:52:30.440 --> 00:52:33.170
It's continuously
running and buying things

00:52:33.170 --> 00:52:36.320
based on that last predicate
that I defined on my own.

00:52:36.320 --> 00:52:39.980
So, the next question is, how do
you actually get the scheduler

00:52:39.980 --> 00:52:42.110
to run inside of Kubernetes.

00:52:42.110 --> 00:52:44.840
So I'm going to kill
it from our laptop,

00:52:44.840 --> 00:52:46.940
and now we need to deploy
it to Kubernetes itself,

00:52:46.940 --> 00:52:49.440
so of course, we need
to deployment object.

00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:51.490
So here, we have a
scheduler object,

00:52:51.490 --> 00:52:55.269
and it needs to run as a pod
itself inside of a cluster.

00:52:55.269 --> 00:52:56.810
And if you've guessed
it, we're going

00:52:56.810 --> 00:52:59.390
to use the default scheduler,
to schedule our scheduler,

00:52:59.390 --> 00:53:02.150
so we can have parallel
schedulers, in our cluster.

00:53:02.150 --> 00:53:02.770
OK?

00:53:02.770 --> 00:53:04.710
So let's see if this works.

00:53:04.710 --> 00:53:06.890
So we see we have some
custom workloads there,

00:53:06.890 --> 00:53:12.870
so we'll say kubectl, create,
dash F, deployment scheduler.

00:53:12.870 --> 00:53:15.557
And at this point,
our scheduler will

00:53:15.557 --> 00:53:17.140
be scheduled by the
default scheduler,

00:53:17.140 --> 00:53:19.000
and now, we have
parallel schedulers

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:20.010
running in Kubernetes.

00:53:20.010 --> 00:53:20.510
Thank you.

00:53:27.630 --> 00:53:28.780
APARNA: That's great.

00:53:28.780 --> 00:53:30.460
Thank you, Kelsey.

00:53:30.460 --> 00:53:32.702
So that concludes the
demos and the features

00:53:32.702 --> 00:53:33.910
that we're going to announce.

00:53:33.910 --> 00:53:36.680
There's a few more
features, obviously,

00:53:36.680 --> 00:53:39.370
and there's a much
bigger roadmap.

00:53:39.370 --> 00:53:42.100
There were other talks
at the conference

00:53:42.100 --> 00:53:45.040
yesterday and today, that
talked about auto-upgrades

00:53:45.040 --> 00:53:47.014
and auto-repair in
Container Engine.

00:53:47.014 --> 00:53:48.430
That's something
that has launched

00:53:48.430 --> 00:53:50.680
as beta as of this conference.

00:53:50.680 --> 00:53:53.410
And then we are also
going to be introducing

00:53:53.410 --> 00:53:56.680
RBAC, roll-based access
controls, as beta.

00:53:56.680 --> 00:53:58.450
And network policy
is already beta.

00:53:58.450 --> 00:54:00.550
And then Container
Runtime Interface

00:54:00.550 --> 00:54:02.800
will be going beta as well
in the next release, which

00:54:02.800 --> 00:54:06.616
allows you a choice of
different run times.

00:54:06.616 --> 00:54:08.240
These are some of
the related sessions.

00:54:08.240 --> 00:54:11.410
But what I'd like to do now,
is to open it up for Q&amp;A,

00:54:11.410 --> 00:54:14.020
and to invite George and
Kelsey to come up and join me

00:54:14.020 --> 00:54:16.125
on stage for your questions.

00:54:22.515 --> 00:54:24.140
KELSEY: Could we give
round of applause

00:54:24.140 --> 00:54:27.670
for Aparna for our
awesome presentation?

